WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.660
When working with data we may rarely if ever&nbsp;
always need every column from a table. And we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:11.520
may not need to see every record from a table.&nbsp;
Elsewhere in this course we will cover the SQL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.920
language needed to select information based on&nbsp;
criteria. Here we see a statement for a view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.540
a view in databases is a saved query,&nbsp;
a view does not store information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:27.120
rather it is a storage statement that leads to&nbsp;
getting specific information. For a real-life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.640
analogy it’s like going to the store and looking&nbsp;
in a specific produce section for apples rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.880
than scanning the entire store for apples. And,&nbsp;
with a view, one does not need to type the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:45.300
SELECT statement every time one wants to see a&nbsp;
specific set of records. The statement starts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:53.580
a CREATE VIEW and the name of the view, then&nbsp;
we see a SELECT statement that selects from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.580 --> 00:01:00.180
but not all the columns from the SALESORDERHEADER&nbsp;
table, and there is a criterion that restricts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.640
query to just show the orders from just the month&nbsp;
of June 2011. You will also notice that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:10.740
a SELECT statement below this with two dashes&nbsp;
in front of it. These two dashes comment out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.380
the statement, so it will not run at this time.&nbsp;
I am going to execute this CREATE VIEW statement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:25.680
And then I will select the&nbsp;
SELECT statement and run it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.460
And we see the columns and rows from the&nbsp;
SALESORDERHEADER table that are included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:40.080
in this view. Altering the view is a far&nbsp;
different process than altering a table.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.500
To alter a view the syntax is basically the&nbsp;
same as it is to create a view except we need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:56.760
to change the keyword CREATE to ALTER. Then I&nbsp;
will add another column to this view, TotalDue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.840
then I will execute this statement and&nbsp;
the view has been altered. Dropping a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.360
view is similar to dropping a table. I want&nbsp;
to drop this sales view test view, I will type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:26.820
DROP VIEW Sales.SaleViewTest to drop the&nbsp;
version of this view which I created earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:34.800
and then I will select and execute the&nbsp;
statement. That view has now been deleted.

